**What changed.**
- The build splits every string under a key naming a provenance out of the published vessels.json into docs/data/provenance.json, keyed by vessel id and the audit's own path grammar. It merges the split back and refuses the build if the result differs from the source.
- The budget gate names the split file as the one data file off the first-paint sum, and checks the served app.js names it exactly once, inside the loader. A fetch added anywhere else puts it back on the bill.
- The app gains an on-demand loader that fetches the file once, merges each string into the record by path, and reports any path that finds no home. Nothing on the boot path calls it.
- The audit awaits that merge before any rule reads the record, and a new rule convicts a served split file that merged nothing or a string whose path finds no home. The audit runner takes its origin from the environment.
- The source file in web/ is untouched, so round scripts and the audit against web/ see the record whole.

**Verified.**
- First paint is 8.41 MB against 8.6, with 0.19 MB on demand.
- The audit passes on 33 hulls against both the whole record and a server on the split docs/ tree. Deleting one provenance from the split file convicts exactly the platform's silence. Pointing one string at a nonexistent mast convicts exactly the new rule.
- The Shipwright captured from the whole and split origins by the same tool is pixel-identical. Harness checks on the shipwright and ship-endurance frames pass.
- The live stamp matched 60 s after the push, and the live data files were read directly. The Endurance record on the live site carries the platform with its provenance in the split file.
