**What was found.** The State Library of Victoria serves every one of its twenty Preussen records at native scan resolution through a Rosetta IIIF manifest. The route runs Primo record → Alma representation → viewer IE → Rosetta image server, and only the JPG derivative is served, not the TIF. Thirteen plates were fetched and looked at on one sheet. The Brodie collection print of her at anchor (H99.220/3712, 5421 px) has the whole hull, every truck and every yard square on the starboard beam at one scale, with no measurable foreshortening.

**What the plate reads.** By r286's method, the three fixed yards on all five masts and the five trucks:

| mast | deck to truck | course | lower topsail | lower topgallant |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| fore | 55.8 m | 0.25 | 0.40 | 0.69 |
| main | 58.0 m (attested) | 0.27 | 0.41 | 0.69 |
| kreuz | 57.1 m | 0.26 | 0.41 | 0.68 |
| laeisz | 53.1 m | 0.22 | 0.38 | 0.67 |
| jigger | 46.6 m | 0.23 | 0.38 | 0.68 |

The split r286 read from the quarter and could not resolve is on this plate too, so it is the ship and the record now carries per-mast fractions. The trucks are the larger finding: the attested 58 m is the main's, and the after masts are shorter, the jigger by eleven metres. The other four heights are recorded as derived from the plate with ±0.5 m bounds.

**What changed.** The record only: five masts' yardFracs and truckM and two provenances. No builder, audit or shader line moved. The shipped audit passes all 33 hulls. Nine frames were scored one at a time; three moved and were accepted (ship-preussen, aboard-preussen, and the dreadnought's frame where Preussen's canvas reaches its edges), six sat inside the gate. The abeam witness shows the after masts lower and the forward courses a little higher, with the card's figure still 58.0 m.

