**What broke, and the structural fix.** The first audit on the new record read 356 problems on Preussen alone. A deck depth turns on the r276 wooden-bulwark furniture, and with pins present every pin-gated gear rule of r276 to r285 ran on her five fidded six-yard steel masts. The r276 furniture models a wooden bulwark by its own comment; a steel bulwark is plating on stays, which the builder has never drawn. The builder now withholds that furniture from steel and iron hulls, the audit's furniture gate carries the same case, and the record names the stays, pin rails, freeing ports and islands as not drawn.

**Verification.**
- Audit: all 33 hulls pass. Proof C (deck block removed) convicts exactly the new rule, which reads the silence: a rig read over a bulwark's depth on a hull whose deck is drawn at the cap. Proof B (builder ignoring the depth) convicts the r215 rule on Preussen and Endurance.
- Measured: the weather deck 5.00 to 3.65 m, the masts' heels with it, the trucks and every yard 1.35 m lower, the rail, channels and deadeyes unchanged, every yard fraction identical.
- Witnessed abeam and over the rail, before and after at the same cameras. From a boat's height the deck strip disappears behind the bulwark; from over the rail the planked deck lies inside a dark inboard wall.
- Frames: 9 scored one at a time, 3 moved and accepted with reasons (ship-preussen, aboard-preussen, and the dreadnought's frame edges), 6 inside the gate. Every accepted frame was read whole.

**Left for r292, in HANDOFF.md.** (0y¹⁰⁹) the islands every plate shows and the model does not draw, first in order. (0y¹⁰⁸) the steel bulwark's furniture, which waits on generalising the running-gear derivation beyond Endurance's one mast, as a declared two-round pair. (0y¹¹⁰) the depth itself and the unprovenanced freeboard, which a general-arrangement drawing would fix.
