**Queue:** August's second list was already finished, so this round took (0y¹³⁶), the item r308 named first: Titanic's after well deck.

**What was wrong:** r299 traced her C deck (the main deck that forms the floor of both wells) off the 1911 side plan. Each traced point was allowed to sit up to 4 rows from the last one. At column 790 a solid line starts 5 rows below the dashed C line, and the trace slipped onto it and followed it all the way to the stern. r299's own notes said it followed the dashed line; its data shows it did not. That left the after well deck about 0.3 m too low. It also explains about half of r307's puzzle that the after wash ports stood 0.7 m above the deck.

**The fix:**
- **Re-trace:** I traced the line again, allowing each point to move at most 1.5 rows from the last. All 346 points stay on the dashed line, and the fit's error halved (1.20 → 0.60 px).
- **New heights:** the deck in the after well rises 0.27–0.30 m, and the deck under the poop and at the stern rises 0.37–0.42 m. Forward of u 0.70 nothing moves more than 0.06 m.
- **Boat deck kept:** I adjusted the deck spacing so the boat deck stays at its recorded 19.0 m.
- **Code:** only the vessel record changed; the hull builder did not.
- **Measured:** the after well's foot went 9.70 → 9.93 m, the poop's 9.77 → 10.11 m, and the stern's 10.14 → 10.53 m. The house top and the two masthead heights are unchanged.
- **Wash ports:** the after ports now sit 0.35–0.44 m above the deck.

**Audit:**
- **New rule:** the vessel record now keeps the raw trace. The audit flags any trace that jumps more than 1.5 rows between neighbouring points, any fitted point more than 2 px off the trace, and a record with no trace. Fed r299's old trace, it found 4 problems; on the final tree it finds none (33 hulls, 0 problems).
- **Older rule fixed:** the check on the boat deck's height still worked from a default 10.1 m deck height. Since r299 the builder has used the traced deck, so the check had been passing by 0.001 m. It now reads the traced deck.

**Seeing it:** I compared before and after views of Titanic from above her starboard quarter. The inner face of the far bulwark now shows 13 px above the deck where it showed 17–18 px. In the full frame she reads as a rendered liner on a rippled sea, not a chart. You can see her four buff funnels, two open well decks between the forecastle, superstructure and poop, and a black hull with rows of portholes.

**Frames:** all 65 scored.
- **Accepted:** `ship-titanic` and `aboard-titanic` moved as predicted; every changed pixel is on Titanic.
