Round 344 is done and live. The deployed version stamp now reads 1795712261.

**What changed.** The 74's forecastle and quarterdeck walls are now planked. In round 343 they used the flat paint meant for steel ship structures, so up close each read as a painted slab on top of a planked side. On a wooden hull those walls now use the hull's own planking shader. The plank lines, joints and wooden pegs carry on up from the side at the same spacing, in the recorded tarred black. The plain bands of the side's paint scheme stay below the deck edge. Steel ships with raised decks (Preussen, Titanic) are unchanged.

**Checks:**
- **Looked at the before and after frames.** Before, the quarterdeck wall is one flat dark slab. After, it shows plank seams about 0.47 m apart with staggered joints, on both faces.
- **New audit rule, A-ISLAND-PLANKED.** On a wooden hull, the raised decks' walls must use the planking shader, and their plank pattern must line up with the side's where the two meet.
  - It flagged all four wall meshes on round 343's code.
  - It also flagged all four on a deliberately broken version that restarts the plank pattern from the waterline.
  - The first version of the rule missed the two end walls in that test; I fixed it before the final run.
  - The final audit passed: 33 hulls, 0 problems.
- **Frame comparison.** I re-rendered the 22 test frames that can show the 74. Four changed, all predicted and each looked at: `shipwright` 0.088%, `shipwright-astern` 0.477%, `shipwright-furled` 0.468%, `shipwright-hounds` 0.107%. In each, the only change is the plank lines on those walls. I accepted them with written reasons.
  - `aboard-coast` read 17.996%. That is a known rendering glitch that shows up when two browsers capture at once, and it repeats to the same value. Recaptured alone it scored 0.032%, so I did not accept it.
  - The other 43 frames cannot show the 74 and were not re-rendered.

**Not done:**
- Item 1 also asked me to read the raised-deck walls' decorative mouldings off Eckersberg's 1828 painting of the *Asow*. I did not, and I did not draw the forecastle's panelling that the same painting shows.
- The inside face of each wall uses the outside paint. The real inside colour, often red on Royal Navy ships, is not in the ship's data.

Both are recorded in HANDOFF.md, and the mouldings and panelling are item 3 of the queue for round 345. Item 1 there is the second capstan in the open middle deck (the waist). The model's only capstan is now hidden under the quarterdeck.
